A1. What golf players have to be in order to go to heaven. ~ DEAD
A4. Something all wives can do to their husbands (two words.) ~ OUTTALK
A9. Used by gamblers for a genuine clean-up. ~ SOAP
A12. Some girls are called Spearmint because they are wriggly and always after these. ~ MEALS
A14. This is the bunk on a Pullman car. ~ BEZTH
A16. Preposition. ~ TO
A18. A flowery beginning. ~ BUD
A19. A bachelor's button. ~ PIN
A21. Merry. ~ GAY
A22. The skeptics state (abbr.). ~ MO
A23. Verb meaning to get outside of. ~ EAT
A25. Modern Romeos. ~ PAPAS
A27. What all flat tires get. ~ AIR
A28. A sheik's work bench (pl.). ~ SETTEES
A29. There's not much meat in these kind of stories. ~ SERIALS
A30. What eskimos have to do in order to go to heaven. ~ DIE
A31. What grandmother said when she hit her thumb with the hammer. ~ PSHAW
A33. Something modern girls do best. ~ PET
A35. A form of "to be." ~ IS
A36. A feminine persuader. ~ SOB
A38. Another great benefit derived from crossword puzzles is learning that this is a fish. ~ EEL
A39. The girl whose whereabouts has caused so much speculation. ~ SAL
A41. A means of transportation (abbr.). Ask any railroad president. ~ RZ
A42. This makes people hungry. ~ TONIC
A44. The only kind of a law married people fear. ~ INLAW
A46. This means "place" or "stead" in French. ~ LIEU
A47. This kind of language flows in a golf stream, ~ PROFANE
A48. An old time sheik. ~ BEAU
A49. A feline "lover's lane." ~ ALLEY
A51. Small boy's favorite style of swimming. ~ NAKED
A53. A parent. ~ MA
A55. Sorrowful. ~ SAD
A56. Interjection of cold bath liars. ~ BRR
A58. Kidders Exterminator's League (abbr.). ~ KEL
A59. A part of "to be." ~ BE
A60. Something we like about a modern girl. ~ ARM
A62. To place again. ~ RESET
A64. More dangerous to step on than a rattlesnake. ~ GAS
A65. What wives say when they leave their husbands. ~ GOODBYE
A66. The only thing that women will follow consistently. ~ FASHION
A67. One way to collect what's coming to you. ~ DUN
A68. What Napoleon did when he tried to conquer Russia. ~ ERRED
A70. Held by investors in wild-cat stock. ~ BAG
A72. A note of the scale. ~ RE
A73. One of Ham's partners. ~ EGG
A75. A title of respect. ~ SIR
A76. Indisposed. ~ ILL
A78. A feminine "may be." ~ NO
A79. What you are apt to find artists' models in if you enter a studio without knocking. ~ POSES
A81. This is hot stuff. (Don't ask a janitor, he won't know!) ~ STEAM
A83. Something people get in the neck. ~ PAIN
A84. These are taboo in Bryan's zoo. ~ MONKEYS
A85. Mr. Volstead is trying to do this to us. ~ WEAN

D1. Flapper's favorite fruit. ~ DATE
D2. Form of the verb "to be." ~ AM
D3. Society bait (abbr.). ~ DEB
D4. Automobiles keep people from getting this way. ~ OLD
D5. Pronoun. ~ US
D6. You can't go far on your vacation without taking one of these. ~ TRIP
D7. Fourteen ounces to a butcher (abbr.). ~ LB
D8. A good thing to tap for spirits. ~ KEG
D9. An eye-sore. ~ STY
D10. Exclamation. ~ OH
D11. The way a woman keeps a man. ~ POOR
D13. A kind of bridge. ~ AUCTION
D15. Anyone whose political opinions differ from ours. ~ ZADICAL
D17. A wet spot in a dry country. (Nothing at all to do with drug stores!) ~ OASIS
D19. This means out of date like long hair and clothes. ~ PASSE
D20. That Parisian accent. ~ NASAL
D22. A nickel nurser. ~ MISER
D24. A little hill on a golf course. ~ TEE
D25. Something all old men try and show. ~ PEP
D26. Something bachelors have to learn to do. ~ SEW
D27. Switzerland is famous for cheese, yodlers and this. ~ ALP
D30. What some boys get out of college. ~ DIPLOMA
D32. Young contented cows. ~ HEIFERS
D34. Things that actors travel in. (Not freight cars or patrol wagons.) ~ TZOUPES
D36. These are things that go up on high. ~ SOULS
D37. This is what man is because he only has to buy one pair of shoes at a time. ~ BIPED
D39. Webster says this is one who is guilty of underhand work. ~ SNEAK
D40. You'll often find one of these sticking around a bottle of gin. ~ LABEL
D42. A cat fight. ~ TEA
D43. Something both big and little babies do. ~ CRY
D44. The lair of the modern highway robber. ~ INN
D45. What bachelors get when they decide to give up their life of ease. ~ WED
D50. Social substitutes for children. ~ LAPDOGS
D52. There's nothing more useless than a glass eye at one of these. ~ KEYHOLE
D54. It's quite useless to do this with a woman. ~ ARGUE
D56. These used to be sent to us in schooners. ~ BEERS
D57. To trace or carry back. ~ REFER
D59. An Italian vender's word for banana. ~ BANAN
D61. That blue day (abbr.). ~ MON
D62. A scarce beverage in the land of the spree. ~ RYE
D63. A kind of a pole. ~ TAD
D64. A sailor. ~ GOB
D67. What bricklayers do when they fall off a scaffold. ~ DROP
D69. A dangerous thing to run. ~ RISK
D71. An evening dress. ~ GOWN
D73. A long, long time. ~ EON
D74. A precious stone. ~ GEM
D76. Possessive pronoun. ~ ITS
D77. One thing flappers do not want to get into the hands of. ~ LAW
D79. An appetizing Greek letter. ~ PI
D80. In like manner. ~ SO
D81. The first name of all union rubes. ~ SY
D82. Pronoun. ~ ME


Misprints, kept as printed (printed → meant):
A47: This kind of language flows in a golf stream, → golf stream.
D27: Switzerland is famous for cheese, yodlers and this. → yodlers
D37: This is what man is because he only has to buy one pair of shoes at a time. → at

